# Prompt Pilot — Phase Governance

## Required process for every implementation phase

1. Audit current repository state.
2. Read the relevant contracts.
3. Confirm prerequisites.
4. Mark only the approved phase `IN_PROGRESS`.
5. Implement only the approved scope.
6. Add or update focused tests.
7. Run focused tests.
8. Run relevant regression tests.
9. Run typecheck.
10. Run build when a build script exists.
11. Run `git diff --check`.
12. Update [docs/implementation-checklist.md](docs/implementation-checklist.md).
13. Create one approved local commit.
14. Confirm `git status --short` is clean.
15. Mark the next phase `READY`.
16. Stop without implementing the next phase.

## Execution policy

- Cursor performs all testing and verification.
- Never ask the user to run normal project tests manually.
- Never run two phases together unless a future phase contract explicitly authorizes a combined pack.
- Stop after one repeated failure and report the root cause.
- Do not weaken tests to obtain a passing result.
- Do not auto-commit partial or failing work.

## Prohibited without explicit user approval

- `git push`
- Adding a Git remote
- Creating a GitHub repository
- Deploying to Vercel, Supabase cloud, Render, or any production infrastructure
- Publishing packages or public preview deployments

## Repository safety

- Do not perform destructive Git commands (`git reset --hard`, `git clean -fd`, history rewrite).
- Do not amend earlier commits unless explicitly authorized.
- Preserve user files.

## Scope control

- Future ideas belong in the roadmap or checklist notes, not the current implementation.
- Do not modify unrelated modules while editing nearby files.
- Do not silently change product contracts.
- Exactly one phase may be `IN_PROGRESS` at a time.

Status tracking: [docs/implementation-checklist.md](docs/implementation-checklist.md) is authoritative for execution status; [docs/phase-roadmap.md](docs/phase-roadmap.md) describes scope.
